# ── AbeerSuite Counter Machine Environment — OPTIONAL OVERRIDE ───────────────
# You do not need this file. A counter installs and runs unattended: the installer
# already carries its own configuration at `resources/counter.env`, written at build
# time from the allowlist in scripts/counterEnv.ts.
#
# This file exists for the exception — pointing ONE machine somewhere else without
# building a separate installer. Copy it to that counter's app-data folder as `.env`:
#
#     %APPDATA%\abeersuite-electron\.env
#
# The folder name comes from Electron's app.getName(), which reads package.json
# "name" — NOT the "AbeerSuite" product name. If AbeerSuite shows a configuration
# dialog it names the exact path it looked in; trust that path over this comment.
#
# HOW IT WINS: the main process loads this file FIRST and the baked one second, and
# dotenv never overwrites a variable that is already set. So every key you put here
# replaces the baked value, and every key you leave out keeps it. Delete the file to
# go back to the shipped configuration.
#
# Keep it minimal. A key set to a blank or placeholder value here does not fall back
# to the baked one — it shadows it, and AbeerSuite refuses to start rather than run
# with a config nobody can log into. Only write the lines you actually mean to change.
#
# EVERY VALUE BELOW IS A DUMMY. This file is committed to the repository, so never
# paste a real credential into it.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one key worth overriding: point a single counter at a test database while the
# rest keep using the shipped connection. Use the pooler port (6543).
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECTREF:PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&sslmode=require"

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Do NOT set AUTH_SECRET. Each machine generates its own on first run and stores it
# in %APPDATA%\abeersuite-electron\auth-secret.json, which survives upgrades. Sessions
# never leave 127.0.0.1, so counters have nothing to share — and a secret written here
# is a secret sitting in plaintext that also cannot be rotated without a site visit.

# Staff email domains allowed at /staff/login. Shipped in the installer already.
# The check FAILS CLOSED: set this to an empty or wrong value and EVERY staff login
# is refused with "Invalid email or password", before the password is even checked.
# See src/shared/lib/auth/staffEmailAllowlist.ts.
# INTERNAL_STAFF_EMAIL_DOMAINS=yourcompany.in,yourothercompany.in

# Escape hatch for a machine whose database is unreachable or empty. Checked BEFORE
# the domain allowlist and before any database query. Deliberately NOT shipped in the
# installer — it is a full-authority login in plaintext, so add it to the one machine
# being recovered and delete it afterwards.
# SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@yourcompany.in
# SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Counter mode ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# v1 runs in LIVE mode: the counter reads and writes straight to Supabase and there
# is no local replica. Leave COUNTER_PC unset for that.
#
# Setting COUNTER_PC=1 opts this machine into the local-replica path. It only does
# something once portable PostgreSQL binaries are bundled in resources/pgsql — until
# then the app logs a warning and stays in live mode.
# COUNTER_PC=1

# Connection string for the local replica. Leave this unset: the app sets it itself,
# and only after it has confirmed a local PostgreSQL is actually listening. Pointing
# it at a database that does not exist turns a brief Supabase blip into a hard failure.
# LOCAL_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres@localhost:54329/smx

# Where to serve reads from: live | shadow | local
# READ_MODE=live

# ── App mode ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APP_MODE=office

# ── This counter's identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every counter has a distinct EPBX extension already (PosCounter.epbxExtension,
# set in office/admin/counters) — this is that same number, told to Electron so it
# can pick a unique port. The server listens on 3000 + this value (extension "04"
# -> port 3004), bound to the LAN (not just loopback) so any PC on the shop wifi can
# open http://<counter-ip>:<port> in a browser. Staff login is still required.
# Two digits only. Unset -> port 3000, same as before.
# COUNTER_EPBX_EXT=04

# ── Auto-update ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PAT_TOKEN + GITHUB_UPDATE_REPO are baked into every counter already
# (scripts/counterEnv.ts). GITHUB_UPDATE_REPO ("owner/repo") deliberately points
# at a separate, private, release-artifacts-only repo — not the source repo.
# PAT_TOKEN is a fine-grained PAT, "Contents: Read-only", scoped to ONLY that
# repo. Override either here only to point ONE counter's update checks
# elsewhere — e.g. a test build pulling from a different releases repo. Leave
# unset to keep the baked ones. Blank here (not just unset) disables update
# checks on this machine only, same fail-closed rule as every other key in
# this file.
# PAT_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_UPDATE_REPO=
